            "# LangGraph Stateful Workflows\n\n## Graph execution model\nLangGraph represents workflows using nodes and edges. A node executes a unit of application logic and can read or update shared state. Edges determine which node runs next, and conditional routing can choose different branches. Unlike a simple linear chain, a graph can revisit nodes and support iterative behavior. The graph structure makes control flow explicit.\n\n## State and reducers\nState is the data carried between graph steps. A state schema describes the keys used by nodes, while reducers define how updates from multiple executions are combined. Incorrect update semantics can overwrite messages or intermediate results. Engineers should design state as carefully as an API request contract, distinguishing durable facts from temporary computation artifacts.\n\n## Agent tools and iterative loops\nAn agent workflow may ask a model to select a tool, execute the tool, append the result, and return to the model. This cycle can repeat until a completion condition is reached. Explicit routing and iteration limits prevent accidental infinite loops. Tool execution requires application-side validation, timeouts, and permission checks rather than relying on the model to enforce policy.\n\n## Persistence and checkpoints\nCheckpointing stores graph state so execution can resume or continue across requests. A thread identifier separates independent conversations or workflows. Persistent state enables long-running tasks and human review, but also introduces storage, retention, and concurrency concerns. The storage layer must be configured deliberately; in-memory state disappears after a process restart.\n\n## Human review and interruption\nSome workflows need approval before sending an email, updating a record, or performing another consequential action. A graph can pause at an approval boundary and resume after receiving a decision. The approval should be bound to the exact proposed action so later state changes do not silently alter what was authorized. A useful audit trail records the proposal, reviewer decision, and execution result.\n\n## Testing and debugging graph systems\nGraph applications should be tested at node boundaries, routing decisions, and complete workflow level. Inspecting intermediate state helps identify incorrect transitions or lost updates. Common operational failures include repeated tool calls, stale checkpoints, non-idempotent retries, and parallel updates that conflict. Traces should show the path through the graph rather than only the final model answer."
